Precedent P-22: The Apocalypse That Ran On Time
(1999 to 2004)
The Y2K bug was real. Enormous amounts of code stored years as two
digits, wired into banks, power grids, air traffic, and payroll, and at
midnight on January 1, 2000, “00” could mean 1900 to machines that
ran  the  world.  US  GAO,  Year  2000  Computing  Challenge:  Lessons
Learned (2000)
What followed was a global exercise in anticipating failure. Instead of
waiting to see what broke, organizations assumed failure in advance
and worked backward, spending, by common estimates, hundreds of
billions  of  dollars  worldwide  on  auditing,  patching,  and  testing
before  the  deadline.  The  US  governmentʼs  own  retrospective
describes the scale of that work.  GAO, Year 2000 Computing Chal-
lenge
